+# TASK-260924-1nh93t results
+
+## Audit basis and Story base
+
+Read launcher SPEC `0.5.0-draft` §4 from the assigned launcher Story worktree and curator-spec Decision 0018. The module already exposed model/vendor admission, release-pinned permission mapping and conflict inspection, stored-policy inspection, provider-limit verdicts, and plan construction. The sole missing module capability for this launcher contract was a public native-argument classifier that reused those release rows and `internal/nativeargs`.
+
+The task start reminder required verifying the Story base before trusting it. After `git fetch origin main`, the worktree `HEAD`, fresh `origin/main`, and task-board `selected_base_oid` all matched `850ac393250606d57188540ef569103c413bd539` (`main`). No rebase or merge was needed.
+
+## Launcher capability audit
+
+The table covers every §4 behavior that the launcher must obtain from agents-management. The environment-ID aliases, closed mapping to `ax` provider IDs, Curator fragment parsing, launcher defaults and configuration files, channel application, file probes, process handoff, and launcher diagnostics are launcher-owned by SPEC §4.1–§4.7; they do not need duplicate module APIs.
+
+| SPEC clause | Module capability the launcher needs | Public module API | Status |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| §4.1–§4.2 | Resolve the canonical agentic system and its home variable, modes, binary, environment contract, and launch identity. | `agentic.Registry.Lookup`; `agentic.System.ID`, `Capabilities`, `ResolveBinary`, `ChildEnv`; `agentic.Plan` (`Binary`, `Env`, `Home`, `WorkDir`). | Satisfied before this task. The launcher owns Curator env-ID aliases and the env-ID / system-ID / `ax`-provider map. |
+| §4.2–§4.3 | Resolve a runtime/vendor and choose an admitted model and declared effort, including runtime-scoped Pi fallback. | `vendorplugin.Registry.ResolveRuntime`, `RuntimeModels`, `Lineup` / `LineupOf`, runtime declarations and model rows. | Satisfied before this task. The ordered Pi runtime preference is launcher convention; the module supplies each runtime's declared rows. |
+| §4.3–§4.4 | Admit the resolved model/effort pair through the vendor and system boundary; build an interactive plan without bypassing vendor admission. | `vendorplugin.BuildLaunch` / `BuildLaunchWithEnvironment`, `SpawnRequest`, `agentic.LaunchRequest`, `agentic.BuildPlan`; typed admission and launch errors. | Satisfied before this task. |
+| §4.3–§4.4, §4.6 | Map `native` / `yolo`, reject duplicate or conflicting native policy selectors, and bind that grammar to an exact verified tool release. | `LaunchRequest.PermissionMode`, `ToolRelease`, `NativeArgs`; `agentic.LookupReleaseCapability`; plugin-owned release rows and argv builders; `ErrPermissionModeUnsupported`, `ErrPermissionModeDuplicate`, `ErrNativePolicyConflict`, `ErrNativePolicyUnknown`, `ErrPermissionModeUnverifiedRelease`. `BuildLaunch` reaches the production gates. | Satisfied before this task. Unknown/unverified releases fail closed; native mode keeps its verbatim forwarding contract. |
+| §4.3–§4.4 | Establish the running release from the launch environment instead of synthesizing it. | `agentic.ProbeToolRelease`, optional `ToolReleaseProber`, `ErrToolReleaseUndetected`. The local Pi wrapper intentionally has no Pi release probe. | Satisfied before this task. The caller passes an empty release after a failed probe; release-bound yolo/classification refuses. |
+| §4.3, §4.4, §4.6 | Inspect known stored-policy relaxations and distinguish inspected, unavailable, and unsupported sources. | `agentic.InspectStoredPolicy(system, plan)`, `StoredPolicyInspection`, typed support/source results. | Satisfied before this task for declared inspectors. Unsupported is distinct from a clean scan. |
+| §4.4 | Check the same resolved runtime/model/managed-home tuple for provider-limit serviceability; preserve read errors as errors. | `providerlimits.Store.AvailabilityFor`, `VerdictQuery`, `Availability.Serviceable`, state and read-failure evidence. | Satisfied before this task. This is a separate launcher-invoked read; `BuildLaunch` does not do it. |
+| §4.4–§4.5 | Return a value-only plan with argv, child environment, stdin, workdir, and binary; refuse unsupported interactive parameters/composition. | `agentic.Plan`, `BuildPlanWithEnvironment`, `System.ChildEnv`, `ErrCompositionNotInteractive`, `ErrParameterNotInteractive`; `vendorplugin.BuildLaunchWithEnvironment` returns owned environment literals. | Satisfied before this task. The launcher applies fragment channels and does not overlay inherited names under `Plan.Env`. |
+| §4.5–§4.6 | Classify whether the native suffix selects a known non-interactive form so the launcher can resolve silent headless permission defaults. The shared flag parser must keep prompt text after `--` opaque. | **Added:** `agentic.Registry.ClassifyNonInteractiveArgs`; `NonInteractiveArgumentClassifier`; `NativeArgsClassification{Form, Grammar}`; plugin classifiers backed by `internal/nativeargs` and each plugin's `ReleaseCapability` rows. | **MISSING before this task; added and covered below.** Unknown systems and unverified releases fail closed with typed errors. |
+| §4.5–§4.7 | Decode Curator fragments, own `defaults.json` / `ax.json`, compose system-prompt/MCP channels, stat launcher-owned files, select `ax` vs exec, and emit launcher diagnostics. | Launcher-owned per the SPEC; module APIs above provide plans, declarations, and typed plugin failures. | No module capability is required. No duplicate launcher API was added. |
+
+## Public classifier
+
+`agentic.Registry.ClassifyNonInteractiveArgs(systemID, toolRelease, suffix)` resolves the system through the existing registry, then dispatches through the optional `NonInteractiveArgumentClassifier` plugin capability. It copies the suffix before dispatch. The result carries `Form` and the same `permission-grammar-v1` or `permission-grammar-v2` token verified for that tool release; `IsNonInteractive()` is false for an empty form.
+
+| Environment/system | Verified release | Grammar | Form and tested placement rows |
+|---|---:|---|---|
+| `claude-code` | `2.1.261` | `permission-grammar-v2` | print: short flag, long flag with `=`, long flag with a separate prompt token |
+| `codex` | `0.153.2` | `permission-grammar-v2` | `exec` and its `e` alias: command at the start, after an equals-form root option, and after separate-value root options |
+| `pi-native` | `0.84.2` | `permission-grammar-v1` | print: short flag, long flag with `=`, long flag with a separate prompt token |
+
+The plugin packages own their tool-specific spellings. All classifiers stop at the shared `--` boundary. Codex skips root option values whose arity is declared in the pinned plugin grammar before identifying its first command token, so a value equal to `exec` is not treated as a subcommand. A root flag whose arity the classifier does not model returns `ErrNativeArgsClassificationIndeterminate` because its following token could be a value; callers must not treat the error as interactive. The `pi` system is a local-runtime wrapper rather than the native Pi CLI and returns `ErrNativeArgsClassifierUnsupported`; the launcher's Pi mapping uses `pi-native`.
+
+Typed refusals are `ErrUnknownSystem` for an unregistered system, `ErrNativeArgsClassifierUnsupported` for a registered system without the optional capability, `ErrPermissionModeUnverifiedRelease` for an absent or unverified tool release, and `ErrNativeArgsClassificationIndeterminate` when the plugin cannot establish the suffix grammar.
+
+## Tests, mutation evidence, and validation
+
+The public registry entry point is driven with fake argument suffixes; no provider or `ax` process was run. Positive rows cover each mapped environment/form and `=`/separate placement, including Codex's `e` alias and an exec command after `--local-provider`. Negative rows cover each print/exec spelling after `--`, a print-looking token after a prior flag and separator, `exec` as a Codex model value, an unmodeled Codex root flag before `exec` returning `ErrNativeArgsClassificationIndeterminate`, unknown systems, an unsupported wrapper system, empty releases, and unverified releases.
+
+Six one-change mutants were applied and tested in disposable copies, then the copies were removed. All six were killed (the mutant test command exited 1 on the named assertion):
+
+| Mutant | Test that failed | Result |
+|---|---|---|
+| Narrow print classification to exact flag elements, losing `=` recognition. | `TestRegistryClassifiesVersionedNonInteractiveFormsByEnvironmentAndPlacement` | Killed; Claude `--print=true` became interactive. |
+| Narrow the separator stop to work only before any prior flag. | `TestRegistryClassifierStopsBeforePromptText` | Killed; `--print` after `--verbose --` was classified. |
+| Narrow Codex root-option value skipping to `--config` only. | `TestCodexClassifierDoesNotTreatAnOptionValueAsTheExecCommand` | Killed; the `--model` value `exec` became a subcommand. |
+| Narrow unknown-root-flag handling so an unmodeled option is treated as a no-value flag. | `TestCodexClassifierFailsClosedForAnUnmodeledRootFlag` | Killed; the following `exec` was misclassified as a subcommand instead of returning the indeterminate error. |
+| Narrow verified-release admission by rejecting Codex's pinned release. | `TestRegistryClassifiesVersionedNonInteractiveFormsByEnvironmentAndPlacement` | Killed; all Codex positive release rows refused. |
+| Widen release lookup by substituting the pinned Codex release for caller input. | `TestNonInteractiveClassifierFailsClosedForUnknownOrUnverifiedInputs` | Killed; empty and unverified Codex releases were admitted. |
+
+The first probe for the new root-arity mutant used the Codex system package with a test name that lives in `pkg/agentic`; it exited 0 with `[no tests to run]` and is not counted. The corrected public-registry probe in `pkg/agentic` exited 1 as expected and killed the mutant; it was rerun after the indeterminate-error change and again exited 1 as expected. During implementation, focused package runs exited 1 because the Codex argvguard treated the parser helper as a construction site and found a duplicated bypass spelling; the parser now references the existing bypass constant, and both read-only parser helpers have justified allowlist entries. Two full-suite runs also exited 1 because Muse's cross-plugin argv guard interpreted Codex's legitimate `--yolo` native alias as a second Muse construction site. Muse's guard now allows only the Codex alias constant and read-only classifier, with the overlap documented; the focused suite including both plugin guards and the final full suite exited 0. A parallel `golangci-lint run --new ./...` attempt exited 3 because golangci-lint was already running concurrently; its standalone rerun exited 0. The full lint command exited 1 on 20 pre-existing findings in untouched files, while the task diff had 0 new lint issues.
+
